# Analects Book 7 (述而) — draft, second half: units 24–49 = passages 7.18–7.37.
# Token format as the pilot:
#   tokens separated by " | "; a token is  CHARS[PUNCT][=pinyin][/context gloss[//char gloss//char gloss]]
#   a leading "^" breaks the verse line before the token.
# Punctuation is editorial (the edition has none) and always trails the word before it.
# Misprint corrected in the reading text (rule 5b): unit 38 (7.28) the print has 已 for 己 (人潔己以進).
# Glyphs kept as the edition prints them: 恒, 虚, 温, 敎, 祗 (for 祇 qí, 7.34).
# Readings Zhu Xi marks that modern practice does not keep: 葉 shè (7.18; we give yè), 行 xìng (7.24;
# we give xíng), 射 (7.26, 食亦反; we give shè); each note says so once. Kept: 識 zhì (7.27),
# 見 xiàn (7.28), 取 qǔ = 娶 (7.30), 和 hè (7.31), 孫 xùn = 遜 (7.35), 亡 wú (7.25), 誄 lěi, 魋 tuí, 恒 héng.

UNITS_B = [
# ---------------- 7.18 ----------------
dict(pas="7.18", w="葉公=yè gōng/the Duke of Ye — Shen Zhuliang, governor of Ye in Chu//Ye (a county of Chu)//duke (a title he took for himself) | 問/asked about | 孔子=kǒng zǐ/Kongzi | 於/of | 子路，=zǐ lù/Zilu | ^子路=zǐ lù/Zilu | 不/did not | 對。/answer",
  l="The Duke of Ye asked Zilu about Kongzi;\nZilu did not answer.",
  i="The Duke of Ye asked Zilu what sort of man Kongzi was, and Zilu gave him no answer.",
  n="葉公 the Duke of Ye was Shen Zhuliang 沈諸梁, courtesy name Zigao 子高, governor (尹) of the county of Ye in Chu; 'duke' was a title he had no right to (Zhu Xi: 僭稱公; He Yan's Kong Anguo says the same). Zhu Xi reads 葉 shè (舒涉反), the old reading of the place name; standard Mandarin now reads it yè, which we give. 問 X 於 Y is 'to ask Y about X'. 子路 Zilu is Zhong You 仲由 (2.17). Why no answer? Zhu Xi offers two guesses: the Duke did not know Confucius, so he must have asked something he had no business asking; or else a sage's virtue is truly hard to put a name to. Kong Anguo: Zilu did not know how to answer."),
dict(w="子/the Master | 曰：「/said | ^女=rǔ/you (= 汝) | 奚/why | 不/did not | 曰：/say | ^其/his | 為=wéi/being | 人/a man | 也，/(pause: as for) | ^發=fā/rousing himself | 憤=fèn/with eager effort | 忘=wàng/(he) forgets | 食，=shí/to eat | ^樂=lè/taking joy (in it) | 以/and so | 忘=wàng/forgets | 憂，/(his) cares | ^不/(he) does not | 知=zhī/know | 老/old age | 之/(clause marker) | 將=jiāng/is about to | 至/arrive | 云爾。」=yún ěr/so it is — just that//so//thus",
  l="The Master said: “Why did you not say:\nas for the kind of man he is —\nroused to effort, he forgets to eat;\ntaking joy in it, he forgets his cares;\nhe does not know old age is about to come — just so.”",
  i="The Master said: “Why didn't you tell him that he is the kind of man who, once he is set on something, forgets to eat; who is so happy in it that he forgets his troubles; and who never notices that old age is coming on? That is all there is to say of him.”",
  n="女 is rǔ, 'you', the word later written 汝. 奚 'why'. 其為人也 'as for his being a man' (為 wéi): 其 + verb phrase + 也 makes a topic, and what follows says what he is. 發憤 is 'to rouse oneself to effort'; 樂 is lè, 'to take joy'; 將 jiāng 'about to'. 不知老之將至: 之 between subject and verb turns 'old age is about to come' into the object of 知. 云爾 closes a report, 'so it is, just that', adding nothing more. Zhu Xi: when he has not yet got a thing he is roused and forgets to eat; once he has it he delights in it and forgets his cares; between the two he bends to it day after day and does not notice his years running short. He only speaks, Zhu Xi says, of how earnest his love of learning is; but savour it and you see a sage's unceasing purity, beyond anyone else's reach. Variant: He Yan's text reads 老之將至也云爾, adding only a particle."),
# ---------------- 7.19 ----------------
dict(pas="7.19", w="子/the Master | 曰：「/said | ^我/I | 非/am not | 生/born | 而/and so | 知=zhī/knowing | 之/it | 者，/one who | ^好=hào/(I am) fond of | 古，/antiquity | ^敏/quick, urgent | 以/in | 求/seeking | 之/it | 者/one who | 也。」/(assertion)",
  l="The Master said: “I am not one who knew it from birth;\nfond of antiquity,\nI am one who is quick in seeking it.”",
  i="The Master said: “I was not born knowing anything. I love the ancients, and I have gone after what they knew with all the speed I could.”",
  n="生而知之者 'one who is born and knows it' — knowledge without study; 者 makes the whole phrase 'one who…', and 非…者也 'is not one who…'. 好 is hào, 'to be fond of' (Zhu Xi: 去聲). Zhu Xi glosses 敏 as 速 'quick', meaning 汲汲 'pressing eagerly after'. One born knowing, he says, has a clear, bright endowment and sees what is right without needing to learn. He quotes Yin Tun: Confucius, a sage who knew from birth, always called himself fond of learning, and not only to spur others on; what can be known at birth is moral principle, but the rites and music, the names of things, and the changes of past and present must still be learned before one can check them against the facts. He Yan's Zheng Xuan: he says this to encourage men to learn. Variant: He Yan's text reads 敏而求之 'quick, and seeking it'."),
# ---------------- 7.20 ----------------
dict(pas="7.20", w="子/the Master | 不/did not | 語=yǔ/talk of | 怪，=guài/the uncanny | 力，/feats of strength | 亂，/disorder | 神。/spirits",
  l="The Master did not talk of the uncanny, of strength, of disorder, of spirits.",
  i="Strange happenings, feats of brute strength, rebellion, and the doings of gods and ghosts — these the Master would not discuss.",
  n="語 yǔ here is 'to talk about, discuss'. Four single nouns stand as objects of one verb. Zhu Xi: 怪 is the strange and freakish, 力 feats of courage and strength, 亂 rebellion and disorder — none of them the right course of things, so the sage did not speak of them; spirits (鬼神), the traces of creation, are not wrong, but they are hard to make clear unless one has gone to the bottom of principle, so he did not talk of them lightly either. He quotes Xie Liangzuo: the sage spoke of the constant, not the strange; of virtue, not force; of order, not disorder; of men, not spirits. He Yan's Kong Anguo gives examples: strength like Ao's, who pushed a boat over dry land, or Wu Huo's, who lifted a thousand jun; disorder like a minister killing his lord or a son his father; spirits, the business of ghosts and gods — things either of no use to teaching, or which he could not bear to speak of."),
# ---------------- 7.21 ----------------
dict(pas="7.21", w="子/the Master | 曰：「/said | 三/three | 人/men | 行，=xíng/walking | ^必/certainly | 有/there is | 我/my | 師/teacher | 焉。/among them | ^擇/I pick out | 其/their | 善/good | 者/(points) | 而/and | 從=cóng/follow | 之，/them | ^其/their | 不/not | 善/good | 者/(points) | 而/and | 改/correct | 之。」/them",
  l="The Master said: “Three men walking —\nthere is sure to be my teacher among them.\nI pick out what is good in them and follow it;\nwhat is not good in them, I correct.”",
  i="The Master said: “Put me on the road with any two others and I will find a teacher there. What is good in them I take up and follow; what is not good I set right in myself.”",
  n="行 xíng 'to walk, travel'. 焉 'in it, among them' (the fused 於之). 其善者 'their good ones' — 者 turns 'their good' into 'what is good in them'; the second 其不善者 is fronted as topic of 改之. Zhu Xi: of three walking together, one is myself; of the other two, one is good and one bad; I follow the good and correct in myself the bad, so both are my teachers. He quotes Yin Tun, citing 4.17: 'seeing worth, think of being level with it; seeing the unworthy, search within yourself' — then good and bad are both my teachers, and how can advance in goodness ever run out? He Yan's commentary: among three there is no fixed worth or folly; he chooses the good and corrects the bad, and so has no fixed teacher. Variant: He Yan's text reads 我三人行，必得我師焉 'when I walk with two others I am sure to get my teacher'."),
# ---------------- 7.22 ----------------
dict(pas="7.22", w="子/the Master | 曰：「/said | 天/Heaven | 生/brought forth | 德/virtue | 於/in | 予，=yú/me | ^桓魋=huán tuí/Huan Tui — Xiang Tui, minister of war in Song//Huan (from his ancestor Duke Huan)//Tui (his personal name) | 其/(how) then | 如=rú/do with | 予=yú/me | 何？」/what",
  l="The Master said: “Heaven brought forth virtue in me —\nHuan Tui, what can he do to me?”",
  i="The Master said: “The virtue in me is Heaven's work. What can Huan Tui do to me?”",
  n="予 yú 'I, me'. 如 X 何 is a fixed frame, 'what (can one) do with X?'; here 如予何 'what can he do to me?', and 其 adds 'then, after all'. 桓魋 Huan Tui (魋 tuí, Zhu Xi: 徒雷反) was Xiang Tui 向魋, minister of war (司馬) in Song; descended from Duke Huan of Song, he was also called by the clan name Huan. Zhu Xi says only that Tui wanted to harm Confucius, and reads the line: since Heaven has endowed me with such virtue, Huan Tui can do nothing to me — he cannot go against Heaven to harm me. He Yan's Bao Xian: Heaven gave him a sage's nature; his virtue matches Heaven and Earth, all is auspicious and nothing goes against him — hence 'what can he do to me?'"),
# ---------------- 7.23 ----------------
dict(pas="7.23", w="子/the Master | 曰：「/said | ^二三子=èr sān zǐ/my friends | 以/take | 我/me | 為=wéi/as | 隱=yǐn/hiding (things) | 乎？/(question) | ^吾/I | 無/have nothing | 隱=yǐn/hidden | 乎/from | 爾。/you | ^吾/I | 無/have nothing | 行=xíng/(that I) do | 而/and | 不/do not | 與=yǔ/show | 二三子=èr sān zǐ/you, my friends | 者，/(that which) | ^是/this | 丘/(is) Qiu | 也。」/(assertion)",
  l="The Master said: “My friends, do you take me for one who hides things?\nI hide nothing from you.\nThere is nothing I do that I do not show you, my friends —\nthat is Qiu.”",
  i="The Master said: “Do you think I am keeping something back from you? I keep nothing back. Nothing I do is done without your seeing it — that is the kind of man I am.”",
  n="二三子 'you two or three', the Master to his disciples (3.24). 以 A 為 B 'to take A as B' (為 wéi). 乎 in 吾無隱乎爾 is the preposition 'from', = 於. 無 X 而不 Y 者 'there is no X that is not Y': 'nothing I do that I do not show'. 與 is yǔ, which Zhu Xi glosses 示 'to show'; He Yan's Bao Xian takes it as 'to share': 'there is nothing I do that I do not do with you'. 丘 is Confucius naming himself. Zhu Xi: the disciples found the Master's Way so high and deep they could not come near it, and suspected him of keeping something back, not knowing that in a sage action and rest, speech and silence are all teaching. He quotes Master Cheng: the sage's Way is like Heaven; if the disciples truly thought it out of reach, their will to go towards it would slacken, so the sage always stoops to meet them; and Lü Dalin: the sage embodies the Way and hides nothing — like the bright signs of Heaven, all is plain teaching, and it is men who fail to look. Variants: He Yan's text reads 以我為隱子乎 'do you take me as hiding things, sirs?' and 吾無所行而不與 with 所, the same sense."),
# ---------------- 7.24 ----------------
dict(pas="7.24", w="子/the Master | 以/by | 四/four (things) | 敎：=jiào/taught | ^文，/letters | 行，=xíng/conduct | 忠，/being wholehearted | 信。/good faith",
  l="The Master taught by four things:\nletters, conduct, wholeheartedness, good faith.",
  i="There were four things the Master taught: letters, conduct, wholeheartedness, and keeping good faith.",
  n="以 X 敎 'to teach by means of X'; the four follow as a list. 敎 is the edition's form of 教 jiào. Zhu Xi reads 行 xìng, 'conduct' (去聲), a tone distinction modern practice does not keep; we give xíng. He quotes Master Cheng: the Master taught men to study letters and cultivate their conduct and to hold on to being wholehearted and true to their word — and 忠 and 信 are the root. He Yan's commentary: these four have form and substance and can be taken up and taught."),
# ---------------- 7.25 ----------------
dict(pas="7.25", w="子/the Master | 曰：「/said | ^聖人，=shèng rén/a sage//sage//man | 吾/I | 不/do not | 得/get | 而/to | 見=jiàn/see | 之/one | 矣；/(so it is) | ^得/to get | 見=jiàn/to see | 君子/a gentleman | 者，/(as for) | 斯/that | 可/would do | 矣。」/(so it is)",
  l="The Master said: “A sage — I do not get to see one;\nto get to see a gentleman — that would do.”",
  i="The Master said: “A sage I shall never get to see. If I could see a true gentleman, that would be enough.”",
  n="聖人 'the sage' and 君子 'the gentleman' are two grades. Zhu Xi: 聖人 is the name for one whose spirit and clarity are past fathoming; 君子 the name for one whose talent and virtue stand out from the crowd. 不得而見之 'I do not get to see him'; 得見…者 'getting to see…' is a topic, picked up by 斯 'that, then' and judged 可 'would do', as in 5.20 再，斯可矣. He Yan's commentary reads the saying politically: the Master is grieving that the age has no enlightened ruler."),
dict(w="子/the Master | 曰：「/said | ^善人，=shàn rén/a good man//good//man | 吾/I | 不/do not | 得/get | 而/to | 見=jiàn/see | 之/one | 矣；/(so it is) | ^得/to get | 見=jiàn/to see | 有/having | 恒=héng/constancy | 者，/one | 斯/that | 可/would do | 矣。」/(so it is)",
  l="The Master said: “A good man — I do not get to see one;\nto get to see one who has constancy — that would do.”",
  i="The Master said: “A truly good man I shall never get to see. If I could see a man who simply held steady, that would be enough.”",
  n="The same frame as the line before, one grade lower: 善人 the good man and 有恒者 'one who has constancy' (恒 héng, the edition's form of 恆; Zhu Xi: 胡登反). Zhu Xi thinks the words 子曰 here are an intrusion, since the passage runs on as one saying. 恒 is the sense of 'constant and lasting'. He quotes Zhang Zai: one who has constancy does not divide his heart; the good man has set his heart on humaneness and has no evil."),
dict(w="^亡=wú/having nothing | 而/yet | 為=wéi/making as if | 有，/having | ^虚=xū/empty | 而/yet | 為=wéi/making as if | 盈，=yíng/full | ^約/straitened | 而/yet | 為=wéi/making as if | 泰，/ample | ^難=nán/hard | 乎/indeed | 有/to have | 恒=héng/constancy | 矣。/(so it is)",
  l="Having nothing, yet making as if having;\nempty, yet making as if full;\nstraitened, yet making as if well off —\nhard indeed to have constancy!",
  i="People who have nothing and act as if they had plenty, who are empty and act as if they were full, who are pinched and act as if they lived at ease — it is hard for such people to keep steady.",
  n="亡 is read wú here, the same word as 無 'not to have' (Zhu Xi: 亡讀為無). Three lines of one shape, A 而 為 B: 'being A, yet acting B' — 為 wéi 'to make, to act as'. 約 'straitened, poor' against 泰 'ample, lavish'. 難乎…矣 'hard indeed…'; 乎 here only adds weight. Zhu Xi: all three are empty show, and whoever is like this cannot keep to the constant. He quotes Zhang Shi: 'sage' and 'gentleman' speak of learning, 'good man' and 'one with constancy' of native quality; and adds his own comment: the distance between the man of constancy and the sage is vast, yet no one has reached sagehood without starting from constancy — which is why the passage ends by dwelling on it, showing the gate into virtue. He Yan's Kong Anguo: such men are hard to call constant."),
# ---------------- 7.26 ----------------
dict(pas="7.26", w="子/the Master | 釣=diào/fished with a line | 而/but | 不/did not | 綱，=gāng/use a rope-net | ^弋=yì/shooting with a corded arrow | 不/he did not | 射/shoot | 宿。=sù/roosting (birds)",
  l="The Master fished with a line, but not with a rope-net;\nshooting with a corded arrow, he did not shoot at roosting birds.",
  i="The Master would fish with a rod and line, but never stretch a net across the stream; he would shoot birds on the wing, but never one asleep on its perch.",
  n="釣 'to fish with hook and line'. 綱, as Zhu Xi describes it, is fishing with a net hung on a great rope stretched right across the stream (He Yan's Kong Anguo: a great rope across the current with many lines hung from it) — taking everything that swims. 弋 yì is shooting with an arrow tied to a cord of raw silk, so bird and arrow can be drawn back; 宿 sù is a bird roosting for the night. Zhu Xi gives 射 an old reading (食亦反) that modern practice does not keep; we give shè. Both nouns 綱 and 弋 are used here as verbs. Zhu Xi quotes Hong Xingzu: Confucius was poor and lowly when young and sometimes had to fish and shoot to feed his parents and supply the sacrifices; but to take every creature, or catch one off guard, he would not do — here you see a humane man's heart: as he treats creatures, so he treats men; as in small things, so in great."),
# ---------------- 7.27 ----------------
dict(pas="7.27", w="子/the Master | 曰：「/said | ^蓋/there may be | 有/there are | 不/not | 知=zhī/knowing | 而/yet | 作/make things | 之/(it) | 者，/those who | ^我/I | 無/have none | 是/of this | 也。/(assertion) | ^多/much | 聞，/hearing | 擇/I pick out | 其/its | 善/good | 者/(parts) | 而/and | 從=cóng/follow | 之；/them | ^多/much | 見=jiàn/seeing | 而/and | 識=zhì/remember | 之：/it | ^知=zhī/knowing | 之/(of) | 次/the next | 也。」/(this is)",
  l="The Master said: “There may be those who make things without knowing —\nI have none of this.\nHearing much, I pick out the good in it and follow it;\nseeing much, I remember it:\nthis is next to knowing.”",
  i="The Master said: “There are perhaps people who go and make things up without understanding them. That is not my way. I listen widely and choose what is good to follow; I look widely and keep what I see in mind. That is the next best thing to knowing.”",
  n="蓋 opens a guess, 'there may be'. 作 is 'to make, to originate'; Zhu Xi: 不知而作 is to act or make without knowing the principle — reckless making; Confucius says he has never done it, which is modest, but shows too that there was nothing he did not know. He Yan's Bao Xian: many men of the time forced the sense and made up writings recklessly. 識 is read zhì, 'to remember' (Zhu Xi: 識音志，記也). 多聞，多見 echo 2.18. Zhu Xi: what one follows must be chosen, but what one records keeps the good and the bad alike, for comparison; such a man, though he has not yet truly grasped the principle, ranks next to one who knows. 知之次 'the next after knowing it'. Variant: He Yan's text reads 知次也 without the second 之; Kong Anguo: such a one comes next to those who know."),
# ---------------- 7.28 ----------------
dict(pas="7.28", w="^互鄉=hù xiāng/Huxiang — a district//Hu (its name)//district | 難=nán/(was) hard | 與=yǔ/with | 言，/to talk | ^童子=tóng zǐ/a boy (from there)//boy//child | 見，=xiàn/was presented | ^門人=mén rén/the disciples | 惑。/were perplexed",
  l="Huxiang was hard to talk with;\na boy was presented,\nand the disciples were perplexed.",
  i="The people of Huxiang were not easy to reason with. When a boy from there was received by the Master, the disciples did not know what to make of it.",
  n="互鄉 Huxiang is the name of a district. 難與言 'hard to talk with': Zhu Xi says its people were steeped in bad ways and hard to speak to of the good; He Yan's Zheng Xuan, that they spoke wilfully and did not fit the times. 童子 a boy, not yet capped. 見 is read xiàn, 'to be presented, to be received' (Zhu Xi: 賢遍反). 惑 'perplexed': Zhu Xi, the disciples doubted that the Master ought to have seen him."),
dict(w="子/the Master | 曰：「/said | ^與=yǔ/approve | 其/his | 進/coming forward | 也，/(pause) | ^不/do not | 與=yǔ/approve | 其/his | 退/going back | 也，/(pause) | ^唯/only — (Zhu Xi suspects words lost here) | 何/why | 甚？=shèn/(be) so severe | ^人/a man | 潔=jié/purifies | 己/himself | 以/in order to | 進，/come forward | ^與=yǔ/approve | 其/his | 潔=jié/purity | 也，/(pause) | ^不/do not | 保=bǎo/vouch for | 其/his | 往/past | 也。」/(assertion)",
  l="The Master said: “Approve his coming forward;\ndo not approve his going back —\nwhy be so severe?\nA man purifies himself to come forward:\napprove his purity;\ndo not vouch for his past.”",
  i="The Master said: “I approve of his coming to me; that is not approving of whatever he may do once he has gone away. Why be so harsh? When a man has made himself clean to come forward, accept the cleanness he brings; you cannot answer for what he did before.”",
  n="The print has 已, a misprint for 己, corrected here. (人潔己 'a man purifies himself' needs 己 'oneself'; our first collation witness reads 絜己, and the Siku copy confuses 己, 已 and 巳 throughout, as at 4.14.) 與 here is yǔ, 'to grant, approve' (Zhu Xi: 與許也) — not the 與其…寧 'rather than…' of 3.4 and 7.35. 潔 'to clean, put in order' (Zhu Xi: 脩治也); 保 'to vouch for'; 往 'the past, former days' (Zhu Xi: 前日也). Zhu Xi suspects the bamboo slips are out of order: the fourteen characters 人潔…往也 belong before 與其進也, and words may be missing around 唯. Read so: when a man cleans himself and comes, accept only that he has cleaned himself — you cannot answer for the good or ill of his past; approve only his coming to see me, not anything bad he does after he withdraws. Do not chase his past or anticipate his future; when he comes with this heart, simply receive him. He quotes Master Cheng: so broad was the sage in dealing with others. He Yan's Kong Anguo takes 唯何甚 as the Master's rebuke: 'you blame me for seeing this boy — why hate the bad so severely?'; and his Zheng Xuan reads 往 as 'going away': one cannot guarantee how he will act after he leaves."),
# ---------------- 7.29 ----------------
dict(pas="7.29", w="子/the Master | 曰：「/said | 仁/humaneness | 遠=yuǎn/far | 乎/(question) | 哉？/(how) | ^我/I | 欲/want | 仁，/humaneness | ^斯/then, here | 仁/humaneness | 至/arrives | 矣。」/(so it is)",
  l="The Master said: “Is humaneness far away?\nI want humaneness,\nand here humaneness has come.”",
  i="The Master said: “Is humaneness so far off? I have only to want it, and it is already here.”",
  n="乎哉 doubles the question particles: 'is it far — how could it be?', a rhetorical question expecting no. 遠 yuǎn 'far'. 斯 'then, at once' links the wanting to the arriving, and 矣 marks the new state: it has come. Zhu Xi: humaneness is the virtue of the heart, not something outside; let the heart wander off without seeking it and it seems far, but turn back and seek it and it is right here. He quotes Master Cheng: being humane comes from oneself (為仁由己); want it and it comes — what distance is there? He Yan's Bao Xian: the way of humaneness is not far; to practise it is to have arrived."),
# ---------------- 7.30 ----------------
dict(pas="7.30", w="陳司敗=chén sī bài/the Minister of Crime of Chen//Chen (the state)//to manage//defeat, crime | 問：「/asked | 昭公=zhāo gōng/Duke Zhao — of Lu//Zhao (his posthumous name)//duke | 知=zhī/know | 禮/the rites | 乎？」/(question) | ^孔子=kǒng zǐ/Kongzi | 曰：「/said | 知=zhī/(he) knew | 禮。」/the rites",
  l="The Minister of Crime of Chen asked: “Did Duke Zhao know the rites?”\nKongzi said: “He knew the rites.”",
  i="The Minister of Crime of Chen asked whether Duke Zhao had known the rites. Kongzi said: “He did.”",
  n="陳 Chen is the state; 司敗 is an office, the same as 司寇 'Minister of Crime' elsewhere (Zhu Xi; He Yan's Kong Anguo: a grandee of Chen). 昭公 Duke Zhao of Lu, personal name Chou 稠, was practised in the forms of ceremonial deportment and was thought in his day to know the rites, Zhu Xi says — so the minister asked, and Confucius answered so. Duke Zhao was his own former lord, and Lu his home state. Variant: He Yan's text reads 孔子對曰, the formal 'answered' used to a superior."),
dict(w="孔子=kǒng zǐ/Kongzi | 退，/withdrew | ^揖/(the minister) bowed to | 巫馬期=wū mǎ qī/Wuma Qi — a disciple//Wu (in the surname Wuma)//Ma (in the surname Wuma)//Qi (his courtesy name) | 而/and | 進/drew forward | 之，/him | 曰：「/(and) said | ^吾/I | 聞/have heard | 君子/the gentleman | 不/does not | 黨，=dǎng/side with his own | ^君子/the gentleman | 亦/too | 黨=dǎng/takes sides | 乎？/(does he) | ^君/your lord | 取=qǔ/took a wife (= 娶) | 於/from | 吳，=wú/Wu | 為=wéi/being | 同/of the same | 姓，=xìng/surname | ^謂/(he) called | 之/her | 吳孟子。=wú mèng zǐ/Wu Mengzi//Wu (the state)//Meng (eldest)//Zi (the surname of Song) | ^君/your lord | 而/if | 知=zhī/knew | 禮，/the rites | ^孰=shú/who | 不/does not | 知=zhī/know | 禮？」/the rites",
  l="Kongzi withdrew;\nthe minister bowed Wuma Qi forward and said: “I have heard that the gentleman does not side with his own —\ndoes the gentleman too take sides?\nYour lord took a wife from Wu, of the same surname,\nand called her Wu Mengzi.\nIf your lord knew the rites,\nwho does not know the rites?”",
  i="When Kongzi had gone, the minister bowed to Wuma Qi, drew him aside and said: “I had heard that a gentleman does not cover for his own. Does a gentleman take sides after all? Your lord married a woman of Wu, who bore the same surname as he, and to hide it called her Wu Mengzi. If he knew the rites, who does not?”",
  n="巫馬期 Wuma Qi — surname Wuma, courtesy name Qi, personal name Shi 施 — was a disciple (Zhu Xi; Kong Anguo). It is the minister who bows to him and draws him forward (Zhu Xi: 司敗揖而進之). 黨 dǎng: 'to help one another hide wrongs is called 黨' (Zhu Xi and Kong Anguo alike: 相助匿非曰黨). 取 is read qǔ and means 'to take a wife', the word later written 娶. The rites forbid marrying someone of one's own surname, and Lu and Wu were both of the Ji 姬 clan; a wife from Wu should have been styled Wu Ji 吳姬. Calling her 吳孟子 'Wu Mengzi' hid this, Zhu Xi says, as though she were the eldest (孟) daughter of the Zi 子 surname of Song. (Not Mencius.) 君而知禮，孰不知禮 is the same frame as 3.22: 而 between subject and verb marks a supposition, 'if your lord knew…'. Variants: He Yan's text lacks 君子亦黨乎 altogether, so the minister goes straight from 'the gentleman does not take sides' to the marriage; it also writes 娶 for 取."),
dict(w="巫馬期=wū mǎ qī/Wuma Qi | 以/(this) | 告。/reported | ^子/the Master | 曰：「/said | 丘/Qiu | 也/(pause) | 幸，/am fortunate | ^苟=gǒu/if | 有/(I) have | 過，/a fault | ^人/others | 必/are sure to | 知=zhī/know | 之。」/it",
  l="Wuma Qi reported it.\nThe Master said: “I, Qiu, am fortunate:\nif I have a fault,\nothers are sure to know of it.”",
  i="Wuma Qi passed this on. The Master said: “I am a lucky man. If I go wrong, someone is sure to notice.”",
  n="以告 'reported (it)', the object of 以 understood. 丘也幸 'as for Qiu, fortunate': the Master names himself. 苟 'if, if ever'. Zhu Xi: Confucius could not say that he had been covering his lord's wrong, nor could he call marrying one of the same surname knowing the rites; so he took it upon himself as his fault and offered no excuse. He quotes Wu Yu: Lu was the Master's parents' state and Duke Zhao a former lord; the minister had not named the matter but suddenly asked whether the duke knew the rites, so the answer was right as given; when the minister called it taking sides, the Master accepted it as a fault, yet still did not say what the fault was, as if he did not know about Mengzi — a pattern for ten thousand generations. He Yan's Kong Anguo: to conceal the wrongs of one's state is itself the rites; the sage, wise and broad, accepted it as a fault."),
# ---------------- 7.31 ----------------
dict(pas="7.31", w="子/the Master | 與=yǔ/with | 人/others | 歌=gē/singing | 而/and (someone) | 善，/(sang) well | ^必/he would always | 使/have (him) | 反/repeat | 之，/it | ^而/and | 後/only then | 和=hè/join in | 之。/it",
  l="When the Master sang with others and one sang well,\nhe would always have him repeat it,\nand only then join in.",
  i="When the Master was singing in company and someone sang a song well, he would always ask to hear it again before he joined in himself.",
  n="與 yǔ 'with'. 反 'to go back over, repeat' (Zhu Xi: 反復也). 而後 'and only after that'. 和 is read hè, 'to sing in answer, join in' (Zhu Xi: 去聲). Zhu Xi: he had the singer repeat it because he wanted to catch it in detail and take in what was good in it; he joined in afterwards because he was glad to have caught it and wished to show approval. In one small act, he says, you see the sage's ease and sincerity, his modesty and care, and his unwillingness to cover over another's merit. He Yan's commentary: he delighted in its goodness, so he had it sung again and then joined in himself."),
# ---------------- 7.32 ----------------
dict(pas="7.32", w="子/the Master | 曰：「/said | ^文，/(in) letters | 莫/perhaps | 吾/I | 猶/am like | 人/others | 也；/(assertion) | ^躬/in my own person | 行=xíng/acting as | 君子，/a gentleman | ^則/then | 吾/I | 未/have not yet | 之/it | 有/(had) | 得。」/attained",
  l="The Master said: “In letters, perhaps I am about like others;\nliving out the gentleman in my own person —\nthat I have not yet attained.”",
  i="The Master said: “In learning and letters I may be about as good as the next man. But to live as a gentleman in everything I do — that I have not yet managed.”",
  n="Zhu Xi takes 莫 as a word of doubt, 'perhaps': 'in letters, perhaps I am like others' — not beyond them, but able to keep up. 躬 'in one's own person'; 行 xíng 'to practise'. 未之有得 'not yet have attained it': after a negative, the pronoun object 之 comes before the verb (as 1.16 不己知). Zhu Xi: both halves are modesty, but they show how much harder, and more urgent, doing is than saying, and he wants men to work at the real thing. He quotes Xie Liangzuo: in letters even the sage is no different from others, so he did not demur; but living out the gentleman is the way into sagehood, so he did not claim it. He Yan's commentary reads the line differently: 莫 is 無 'not', and 文莫 is like the colloquial 文不 — 'in letters, am I not like others?', that is, in all letters I do not surpass others. Variant: He Yan's text ends 未之有得也; Kong Anguo: to be a gentleman in his own person, he had not yet attained it."),
# ---------------- 7.33 ----------------
dict(pas="7.33", w="子/the Master | 曰：「/said | ^若/as for | 聖/sageness | 與=yǔ/and | 仁，/humaneness | 則/then | 吾/I | 豈=qǐ/how | 敢？/would dare | ^抑/but, only | 為=wéi/doing | 之/it | 不/without | 厭，=yàn/tiring of it | ^誨/teaching | 人/others | 不/without | 倦，=juàn/wearying | ^則/then | 可/(it) can | 謂/be said | 云爾=yún ěr/so — just this//so//thus | 已/and no more | 矣。」/(so it is) | ^公西華=gōng xī huá/Gongxi Hua — Zihua//Gongxi (a double surname)//(Gongxi)//Hua (from his courtesy name) | 曰：「/said | 正/precisely | 唯/this is just what | 弟子=dì zǐ/we disciples | 不/(are) not | 能/able | 學/to learn | 也。」/(assertion)",
  l="The Master said: “As for sageness and humaneness — how would I dare?\nBut doing it without tiring,\nteaching others without wearying —\nthat much can be said, and no more.”\nGongxi Hua said: “Precisely this is what we disciples cannot learn.”",
  i="The Master said: “Sage? Humane? How could I presume to claim either? Only that I work at it and never tire of it, and teach others and never weary of teaching — that much may be said of me, and nothing more.” Gongxi Hua said: “And that is just what we disciples cannot learn to do.”",
  n="若 'as for'; 豈敢 'how would I dare?' (豈 qǐ, a rhetorical 'how'), with the claim left unsaid. 抑 'but, only', turning to what he will admit. 為之 'doing it' (為 wéi): Zhu Xi, practising the Way of humaneness and sageness, and 誨人 teaching others the same. 不厭…不倦 'without tiring…without wearying' — the phrase of 7.2. 可謂云爾已矣 'it can be said so, and that is all': Zhu Xi's Chao Yuezhi calls it a phrase for 'nothing else'. Zhu Xi: sageness is greatness that transforms, humaneness the whole virtue of the heart and the full human Way; but never to tire and never to weary is impossible unless one has them oneself — so the disciples cannot learn it. Chao Yuezhi, quoted by Zhu Xi: men of the time were calling the Master sage and humane, so he declined the names; yet had he only declined, there would be nothing to draw on the world's talents, and sageness and humaneness would become empty vessels no one could reach — so he still placed himself as one who works at them unwearied. 公西華 Gongxi Hua is Gongxi Chi 公西赤, courtesy name Zihua (5.7, 6.3); 正唯 'exactly this'. He Yan's Bao Xian: 'just as you say: we disciples cannot learn even this, let alone humaneness and sageness.'"),
# ---------------- 7.34 ----------------
dict(pas="7.34", w="子/the Master | 疾/was ill | 病，/gravely | ^子路=zǐ lù/Zilu | 請/asked leave | 禱。/to pray | ^子/the Master | 曰：「/said | 有/is there | 諸？」=zhū/such a thing (= 之乎) | ^子路=zǐ lù/Zilu | 對/answered | 曰：「/saying | ^有/there is | 之。/such | ^誄=lěi/the Lei (a text of prayers) | 曰：『/says | 禱/(we) pray | 爾/for you | 于/to | 上下=shàng xià/above and below — Heaven and Earth//above//below | 神祗。』」=shén qí/the spirits of heaven and earth//spirits of heaven//spirits of earth (祗 for 祇 qí) | ^子/the Master | 曰：「/said | 丘/Qiu's | 之/(clause marker) | 禱/praying | 久/(has been) long | 矣。」/(so it is)",
  l="The Master was gravely ill; Zilu asked leave to pray.\nThe Master said: “Is there such a thing?”\nZilu answered: “There is.\nThe Lei says: ‘We pray for you to the spirits above and below, of Heaven and of Earth.’”\nThe Master said: “Qiu's praying has gone on a long time.”",
  i="When the Master was very ill, Zilu asked to offer prayers for him. The Master said: “Is that done?” Zilu answered: “It is. The Lei says: ‘We pray for you to the powers above and below, the spirits of sky and earth.’” The Master said: “I have been praying for a long time now.”",
  n="疾病 'ill, and the illness grave': 病 is the worse stage of 疾. 請 'to ask leave'. 諸 here is the fused 之乎 'it?': 有諸 'is there this?' Zhu Xi: the Master asks whether there is any such principle; He Yan's Zhou Shenglie: whether there is such a practice as praying to the spirits for someone. 誄 lěi (Zhu Xi: 力軌反) is, Zhu Xi says, a eulogy mourning the dead and recounting his deeds; He Yan's Kong Anguo, the title of a collection of prayers. 爾 'you'; 于 = 於 'to'. 上下 Heaven and Earth (Zhu Xi: 上下謂天地): the spirits of Heaven are 神, those of Earth 祇 qí — the edition writes 祗 for 祇, in the text and in Zhu Xi's comment (地曰祗). Zhu Xi: prayer is repenting faults and turning to the good to ask the spirits' help; the sage had no faults to repent and his conduct had long accorded with the spirits — hence 'my praying has gone on long'. He adds that the rites for a dying officer prescribe prayers to the five household spirits, an urgent feeling of servants and sons that need not first be put to the sick man; so Confucius did not refuse Zilu outright, only told him there was no need. Kong Anguo: Zilu missed the point. Variant: He Yan's text reads 丘之禱之久矣."),
# ---------------- 7.35 ----------------
dict(pas="7.35", w="子/the Master | 曰：「/said | ^奢/lavish | 則/then | 不/not | 孫，=xùn/modest (= 遜) | ^儉/sparing | 則/then | 固。/shabby | ^與=yǔ/rather than | 其/(being) | 不/not | 孫=xùn/modest | 也，/(pause) | ^寧/better | 固。」/shabby",
  l="The Master said: “Lavish, and so not modest;\nsparing, and so shabby.\nRather than immodest,\nbetter shabby.”",
  i="The Master said: “Extravagance leads to presumption, thrift to meanness. Better mean than presumptuous.”",
  n="孫 is read xùn here, the word later written 遜 'compliant, modest' (Zhu Xi: 去聲，順也) — not sūn 'grandson'. 固 'mean, shabby' (Zhu Xi: 陋也). 則 'then' joins condition and result. 與其 A 也，寧 B 'rather than A, better B' is the frame of 3.4 (禮，與其奢也，寧儉). Zhu Xi: both lavishness and thrift miss the mean, but lavishness does the greater harm. He quotes Chao Yuezhi: it is said because it had to be, to correct the faults of the age. He Yan's Kong Anguo: both are failings, but lavishness is worse than thrift — the lavish man oversteps his superiors, the thrifty one only falls short of the rites."),
# ---------------- 7.36 ----------------
dict(pas="7.36", w="子/the Master | 曰：「/said | 君子/the gentleman | 坦=tǎn/(is) level | 蕩蕩，=dàng dàng/broad and open//broad//broad | ^小人/the small man | 長=cháng/always | 戚戚。」=qī qī/anxious, distressed//sorrow//sorrow",
  l="The Master said: “The gentleman is level, broad and open;\nthe small man is forever anxious and distressed.”",
  i="The Master said: “The gentleman's heart lies open and level, with room to spare; the small man is never free of worry.”",
  n="Two lines of one shape: subject, then a description ending in a doubled word. 坦 'level, smooth' (Zhu Xi: 平也); 蕩蕩 'broad and wide' (寬廣貌) — doubling an adjective makes it describe a look or manner. 長 is cháng, 'long, always'; 戚戚 'anxious and distressed'. Zhu Xi quotes Master Cheng: the gentleman follows principle, so he is always unhurried and at peace; the small man is driven by things, so he has many worries — and again, citing the Great Learning: 'the heart broad, the body at ease'. He Yan's Zheng Xuan: 坦蕩蕩 is a look of breadth, 長戚戚 a look of much worry and fear."),
# ---------------- 7.37 ----------------
dict(pas="7.37", w="子/the Master | 温=wēn/(was) gentle | 而/yet | 厲，=lì/stern | ^威/commanding | 而/yet | 不/not | 猛，=měng/fierce | ^恭/respectful | 而/yet | 安。/at ease",
  l="The Master was gentle yet stern,\ncommanding yet not fierce,\nrespectful yet at ease.",
  i="The Master was warm but firm, he inspired awe without being harsh, and he was courteous without stiffness.",
  n="Three pairs joined by 而 'and yet', each quality held in check by its opposite. 温 is the edition's form of 溫 'gentle' (1.10 夫子溫良恭儉讓); 厲 'stern, grave' (Zhu Xi: 嚴肅也); 威 'commanding awe'; 猛 'fierce'. Zhu Xi: every man's nature is complete, but the endowment of qi he receives is seldom without a bias; only in the sage was the whole of it perfectly blended, yin and yang matched in virtue, so that this balanced spirit showed in his face and bearing. The disciples watched closely and recorded it in detail — and only one wise enough to know a sage and skilled in describing virtue could have done so; which is why, Zhu Xi says, Master Cheng took these to be Master Zeng's words. The passage closes Book 7."),
]
